### How does partitioningBy work?

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class PartitionDemo {
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Account("SAV2002", "Priya",  new BigDecimal("5000")),
            new Account("SAV2003", "Amit",   new BigDecimal("150000")),
            new Account("SAV2004", "Kavita", new BigDecimal("8000"))
        );

        BigDecimal minBalance = new BigDecimal("10000");

        Map<Boolean, List<Account>> partitioned = accounts.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
                a -> a.balance().compareTo(minBalance) >= 0));

        System.out.println("Compliant: " + partitioned.get(true).size());
        System.out.println("Below minimum: " + partitioned.get(false).size());
    }
}
```

```text
Compliant: 2
Below minimum: 2
```

### How is this different from groupingBy?

| Feature | partitioningBy | groupingBy |
|---------|---------------|------------|
| Keys | Exactly 2 (true, false) | 0 to N |
| Both keys guaranteed | Yes | No |
| Classifier type | Predicate (boolean) | Function (any type) |
| Use case | Binary split | Multi-category |

```java
// groupingBy: keys only exist for groups that have elements
Map<String, List<Account>> byHolder = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Account::holder));
// Only keys: Rajesh, Priya, Amit, Kavita
```

### How do you use a downstream collector?

```java
// Count per partition
Map<Boolean, Long> counts = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
        a -> a.balance().compareTo(minBalance) >= 0,
        Collectors.counting()));
// {true=2, false=2}

// Sum balance per partition
Map<Boolean, Double> sums = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
        a -> a.balance().compareTo(minBalance) >= 0,
        Collectors.summingDouble(a -> a.balance().doubleValue())));
```

### What are common mistakes?

WRONG: Assuming groupingBy with a boolean classifier is the same as partitioningBy.

```java
// groupingBy with Boolean: may MISS the false key if all elements are true
Map<Boolean, List<Account>> wrong = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        a -> a.balance().compareTo(minBalance) >= 0));
// If all accounts are compliant: {true=[...]} -- false key MISSING!
```

CORRECT: partitioningBy guarantees both keys.

```java
Map<Boolean, List<Account>> correct = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(
        a -> a.balance().compareTo(minBalance) >= 0));
// Always: {true=[...], false=[]} -- false key present even if empty
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The guaranteed presence of both keys matters for downstream code that does `result.get(false).size()` without null checks.
- partitioningBy creates a `Partition` (internal Map implementation) that always has exactly two entries, making it slightly more memory-efficient than a HashMap.
- For more than two categories, use groupingBy. For boolean splits, always use partitioningBy.
